{"cells":[{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"c1","metadata":{},"source":"## Two tables from one list

`enumerate(chars)` yields `(position, character)` pairs. `stoi` keeps them as `character:position`. `itos` keeps them as `position:character`. Change the list below if you like."},{"cell_type":"code","id":"c2","metadata":{},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null,"source":"chars = ['\n', ' ', '!', 'A', 'a']

stoi = { ch:i for i,ch in enumerate(chars) }
itos = { i:ch for i,ch in enumerate(chars) }

print(stoi)
print(itos)
print(itos[2])"},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"c3","metadata":{},"source":"The two lines differ only in `ch:i` versus `i:ch`. So every key in one table is a value in the other, and the reverse is exact. `itos[2]` gives `'!'`, the character whose position is 2. Here `stoi['!']` is 2."},{"cell_type":"code","id":"c4","metadata":{},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null,"source":"# check the reversal: going through both tables returns where you started
print(all(itos[stoi[c]] == c for c in chars))
print(all(stoi[itos[i]] == i for i in range(len(chars))))"},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"c5","metadata":{},"source":"Try it: add a new character to `chars`, rerun the first code cell, and see both tables grow by one entry. Then rerun this check."}],"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"name":"python","display_name":"Python (Pyodide)","language":"python"},"language_info":{"name":"python"}},"nbformat":4,"nbformat_minor":5}
